WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.499
Thanks to everybody for coming.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:03.779
My name is Stephen Naron and

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.999
I'm the director of the Fortunoff Video Archive

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.410
for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.199
The Fortunoff Video Archive, which began

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.120
taping testimonies in New Haven back in

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.579
1979, contains more than

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.460
4,400 testimonies of survivors,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.469
witnesses and bystanders of the Holocaust.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.833
It encompasses something like 12,000

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.009
hours of recorded material in over a dozen

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.215
different languages recorded over the last 40

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.509
years in over a dozen different countries.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.481
Today, in honor of Black History Month,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.089
we intend to examine one of those testimonies

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.952
in the collection, the testimony of an

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.629
African-American veteran of the Second World

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.179
War, Leon Bass, who entered the camps

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.380
shortly after liberation.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:46.878
I will be doing this with the help of our

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.190
speaker this afternoon, Anna Duensing.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.597
Anna is a doctoral candidate in a

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.569
joint program for history and African-American

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:54.809
studies at Yale University.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.279
Her research, writing, and teaching focuses

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.583
on the United States in the world, specializing

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.717
in race and empire in modern U.S.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.059
history, African-American history,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.249
transnational and global history.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.759
And her dissertation, titled Strange Victory:

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.249
Cold War, Civil Rights, and the Long Shadow of

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.709
Fascism, explores antifascist

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.024
thought and action in the 20th century Black

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.457
freedom struggle, Black radical theories of

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.688
fascism in the United States, and the role

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.609
of the far right with an opposition to the civil

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.610
rights movement.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.069
Today, Anna will be speaking on racism, fascism

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.289
and the Jim Crow military as seen through the

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.660
testimony of Leon Bass.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.269
Anna, we're delighted you're here.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.550
You have the floor.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.759
Thank you so much, Stephen, for the

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.349
invitation to give this talk.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.959
Thanks everybody, who unfortunately I cannot

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.185
see, who has logged in to

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.700
listen. I realized this morning

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.159
that it has been exactly one year

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.251
to the day that I was last in New Haven

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.589
and on campus at Yale.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.072
And, well, I'm sorry

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.529
we can't all be together in person.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.050
And while I recognize the gravity of

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.649
the impending one year milestone of this

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.904
catastrophic pandemic, I'm nonetheless glad to

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.340
be here with you all.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.159
And I also welcome anyone who's

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.463
joining us from the greater, beyond, beyond New

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.990
Haven that is able to join us in the

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.010
setting.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.509
So I'm going to try to speak for around 30

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.029
minutes and also show around 10

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.159
minutes of clips before opening it up to

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.390
questions and comments.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.667
As Stephen Naron said, I've built this talk

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.574
around the story and the experiences

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.789
of an African-American World War II veteran

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.652
named Leon Bass, whose oral history is

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.049
housed in the Fortunoff collection.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.839
And Bass was among the soldiers who participated

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.143
in the liberation of Buchenwald, which was just

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.399
outside the German city of Weimar.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.159
And much of his testimony centers around

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.153
that unexpected and life altering act

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.860
of eyewitness.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.629
But he contextualizes that experience within

00:02:57.639 --> 00:02:59.499
memories of racial oppression in the United

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.959
States, notably what it was like to

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.059
serve in a segregated army,

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.546
relegated to an all-Black labor unit when he

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.160
stepped through the gates of Buchenwald.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.733
And after getting out of the army--army, Bass

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.959
became an educator and eventually a high school

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.750
principal in the Philadelphia area.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.567
And you can really see that expertise,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.829
that demeanor, the demeanor of a lifelong

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.459
educator in every aspect of his testimony

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.279
and his commanding presence,

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.769
which you'll see is solemn but also really

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.583
affable, his storytelling is powerful

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:33.910
and accessible language.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.850
But most of all, his steadfast commitment

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.740
to teach. I'm really trying

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.380
to honor all of that in this talk.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.869
And I'm going to draw on his testimony, but

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.090
threaded together with it with a bigger history

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.179
in order to tell a story about Jim Crow America

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.669
in the 1930s and '40s, about

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.924
young soldiers navigating the racism and other

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.929
indignities of the Jim Crow army.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.299
But more so, I want to talk about how

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.358
African-Americans responded to the rise of

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.189
fascism in Europe, responded to

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.169
Nazi Germany in particular, and responded

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.259
to the escalating policies of antisemitism and

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.729
violence under that regime.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.639
And then toward the end of the talk, returning

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.829
to the testimony of Leon Bass, I'll conclude by

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:17.319
discussing in brief how African-Americans

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.350
responded to the Holocaust and how it informed

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.139
their understanding of ongoing racial violence

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.345
and white supremacy in the United States, and

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.375
also set the stakes for the postwar

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:29.399
Black freedom struggle.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.539
And I do want to note that in this talk, I'm

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.189
going to be showing one photograph taken during

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.169
the liberation of Buchenwald that features the

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.430
corpses of victims.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:38.589
I'm also going to be showing two political

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.170
cartoons that depict lynching,

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.570
the victims of lynching in order to critique it.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:46.560
So.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.110
In September 1933, mere

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.329
months after the Nazis had come to power,

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.639
a foreign correspondent for the Baltimore

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.845
Afro-American wrote an article weighing in on

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.283
the public debates over the escalating rhetoric

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.320
and racial policies of the Nazi dictatorship.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.749
For the past, for the past few months, a

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.729
controversy on the Jew in Germany has been

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.979
raging, he wrote, and it was inevitable that the

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.410
Negro in America should be projected onto it.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.199
He was referencing debates in the Paris edition

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.258
of the Chicago Tribune that juxtaposed the

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.274
situation of German Jews to those of

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.680
African-Americans in the Jim Crow South.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.139
The article meant to intervene in this

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.470
comparison, differentiating between the Black

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.729
experience of racial oppression in the United

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.280
States and the status of Jews confronting,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.369
in his words, the intensely narrow nationalism

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.829
and antiquated theories of race promoted

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.110
by Adolf Hitler.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.000
First of all, this journalist argued,

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.132
Hitlerism appeared to enforce a vastly

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.100
different social order than US race hate.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.962
Nazi Germany lacked the anti-Blackness and color

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.559
lines that criss crossed every aspect of life

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:52.560
back home.

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.819
In his words, the German Jew was also largely

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:57.878
indistinguish-- indistinguishable from the

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.731
so-called Aryan German, and Black residents,

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.084
tourists, and students moved freely throughout

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.288
Germany as they did throughout the rest of

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:06.630
Europe.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.768
In this way, he argued, the persecution on

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.699
the rise in Germany was not strictly about race

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:13.392
as he understood the term.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:16.099
He noted that the majority of Germans being sent

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.380
to the newly constructed concentration camps

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.230
were overwhelmingly socialists and communists.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.709
Comparison slipped too quickly into competition,

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.379
he remarked. In the Jim Crow racial regime

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.949
was a singular monstrosity.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.734
However, he admitted there was no denying that

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.020
Hitler's talk sounds strikingly like the

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.530
frothings of a Southern Cracker.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.639
I want to hone in on this idea of comparisons

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.189
between Jim Crow and Nazism being inevitable,

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.339
as well as the nature of the particular

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.499
comparison and sort of compare and contrast that

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.599
this journalist carried out in this very early

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.059
moment in the Nazi regime.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.129
The Black press had honed in on the grammar of

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.188
race and nation undergirding fascism while

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.727
covering Italy in the decade prior.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.302
Hitler's rise to power only fueled the

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.383
comparative editorial fire, which not

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.579
only noted shared affinities and agendas,

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.279
and made distinctions in policy, ideology

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.995
and national context, but they also

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.009
tracked cross currents of admiration, imitation,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.769
and direct exchange between right wing state

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.410
and non-state actors throughout the interwar

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:21.840
era.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.396
African-American perspectives on fascism and

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.953
antifascist resistance in the 1930s and '40s

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.919
are a crucial resource for us in the ongoing

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:32.469
contemporary debates over fascist analogies

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:34.840
and the fascist potential of the United States.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.899
This historical archive is rich, offering

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.539
a cumulative judgment that fascism and power

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.500
in Europe and exclusionary nationalistic

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:46.329
militant white race pride in the United States

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.690
share common roots, enemies and ambitions.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.667
For many white Americans, fascism initially

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.419
offered a kind of screen upon which they could

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.909
project their own anxieties about US social,

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.679
economic, and race relations.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.739
Depending on their politics then, they might

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:05.019
celebrate or denounce relative proximity.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.379
By contrast, early African-American assessments

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:11.380
of fascist parties, movements, and regimes

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.896
by both radical intellectuals and in popular

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.549
discourse, they, this

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.873
discourse functions more like an indictment and

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.276
a warning, not only about what was happening

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.758
abroad, but also the implications of those

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.999
developments for their own lives.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.840
African-Americans recognize the centrality of

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.899
racial antisemitism to Nazi politics well

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.999
before their white counterparts, and they were

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.058
quick to frame the fascist embrace of race

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.219
and nation as nothing new.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.114
And indeed, soon enough, many Black activists,

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.840
intellectuals, journalists, and everyday people,

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.394
including the journalist I quoted at the start

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.606
of this talk, they arrived at the conclusion

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.069
that these comparisons and connections were

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.649
inevitable because Jim Crow and fascism were

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.289
undeniable bedfellows.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.179
In turn, they signaled alarm over what

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.850
they perceived as dangerous new precedents

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.760
of racialized citizenship, social control,

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.549
state violence, and patriotic militarism

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.159
that would fundamentally transform the world as

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.160
they knew it.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.690
Assessing the historical archive,

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.659
African-American responses to European fascism

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.620
and the Holocaust are a mixture, a broad

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.759
mixture of moral outrage, grassroots

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.070
movement building, political opportunism,

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.979
and anxious concern for the future, for humanity

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:26.980
as a whole.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.516
For race conscious African-Americans engaged

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.255
in a growing Black public sphere of the 1930s,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.355
sensitivity to the race thinking and imperial

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.159
ambitions undergirding fascist politics

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.099
threaded together these complex and sometimes

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.660
contradictory responses.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:44.907
In early 1936, the great poet and

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.419
major public figure Langston Hughes expressed

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.399
the sentiment that bound these manifold

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.390
viewpoints together when he said fascism

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.679
is a new name for that kind of terror the

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.840
Negro in America has always faced.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.299
This kind of terrorism is extending more and

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.729
more to groups of people whose skins are not

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:03.730
black.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.949
Fascism proved a flexible and highly potent

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.023
point of reference for African-Americans.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.697
They approached fascism as an arsenal, as a

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.899
powder keg really, of ideas,

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.359
cultures, agendas and tactics

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:19.580
rooted in racial colonial violence.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:22.429
These social and political forces to them were

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.919
already in circulation, already at work in their

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.190
own communities and in their own histories,

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.259
yet newly fortified and fanatic in the context

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.220
of widespread social conflict and global

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:33.739
economic collapse.

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.949
The scholar Michael Rothberg describes this work

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.557
in the postwar era as multidirectional memory,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.439
the idea that historical actors weren't merely

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.929
comparing events and atrocities in a competitive

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.841
struggle for recognition, but rather as

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.119
a way to understand their worlds and better

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:51.865
articulate their own experiences of

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.619
racial violence and state oppression.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.219
Black theories of and responses to fascism

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.799
in the 1930s and '40s, again from

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.858
a place of relational political engagement

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.716
with Europe, would argue that Europe was not

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.258
the sole entry point for understanding and

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:09.659
combating fascism.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:12.515
This discourse tended to follow a calculus of

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.299
family resemblance, wary of fascism's

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.049
capacity to travel and adapt to diverse

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.220
national contexts.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.874
And here, I think, is a good example

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:25.933
of the kind of comparative relational work that

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:27.799
I'm describing, a rhetorical move that was

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.589
pretty common across the interwar and war time

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:32.239
eras. On the left,

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:35.247
you can see an anonymous woman slain by the

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:37.729
Nazis with reference to the Warsaw ghetto.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.421
And on the right, you see the body of Cleo

00:11:40.527 --> 00:11:42.487
Wright who was lynched by a white mob in

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:44.659
Sikeston, Missouri, in early

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.869
1942 after assaulting a white woman in her home.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.149
And this lynching had garnered international

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.520
attention because it was the first to occur

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:55.099
after the United States had entered the war.

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.009
And because of this added pressure

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:00.068
of the global eye, was also the first case

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.686
of lynching taken up by the US Department of

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:03.720
Justice.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.379
The same day that this cartoon ran, however,

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:08.809
the Black newspaper, The Pittsburgh Courier,

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:11.179
published another compare and contrast piece

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.625
by a white liberal that argued the difference

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.700
between Nazi terror and Jim Crow persecution

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:18.723
was less in-kind and more in terms of

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.039
historical, the historical direction of

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.419
each social order.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.608
So in the words of this writer, Hitler has

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:29.919
crassly reversed the clock of civilization,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:32.575
whereas we in America have been moving slowly

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.232
toward more tolerance.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:38.210
These debates in the Black press, in the Black

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.769
public sphere, were not preoccupied with

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.828
taxonomy, but rather with the vicissitudes

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.139
of the world in which African-Americans lived,

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.149
viewed against the backdrop of

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.419
dictatorship, total war and genocide

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.420
in Europe.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.379
They show a complex combination of sympathy and

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.380
self-interest.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.349
They often sought to highlight American

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.320
hypocrisy and frame Jim Crow as a backwards

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:02.900
anti-democratic system.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:05.301
They aim to expose the very real models

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:08.519
that the Italian fascists had in the European

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.333
scramble for Africa, or the Nazis had

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.158
in US settler colonialism and the Jim Crow

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.530
South.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:17.212
In response to the passage of the Nuremberg laws

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:19.600
in 1935, for instance, one

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.940
journalist wondered, "what are Jim Crow

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:23.989
laws but fascist laws?"

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:26.919
Likely unaware of the extent of efforts that

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:28.978
Nazi lawyers had taken to survey US racial

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:32.259
legislation, he ventured, "it is difficult

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:34.659
to believe that Hitler, to save time,

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.119
did not copy them directly from the Southern

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:39.609
statutes and from the unwritten laws

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:41.589
of America against Negroes."

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.262
In other moments, this connective work

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:46.779
manifested as grassroots global solidarity,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.359
reformulating one group's freedom struggle

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.909
toward a more universal politics, as was

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.189
the case with the global Black-led mass movement

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:56.542
that rose in response to the Italian invasion of

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.469
Ethiopia in 1935.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.609
In other moments in public discourse on fascism,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.560
African-Americans arrived at the idea that there

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.893
was a unique form of US fascism, born of racial

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.060
slavery and its many legacies.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.029
This was often called our native brand of

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:15.669
fascism, and this brand came in forms

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:18.580
as varied as lynch mobs, the Klan, the police,

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:21.279
strike breaking, union busting and other attacks

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:23.709
on labor. The South, as you can imagine,

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.239
very often took center stage.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.979
But their assessments also acknowledge the

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:28.940
United States as a whole with its faulty

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.533
democracy and increasingly domineering presence

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:32.890
on the world stage.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.009
Black antifascist discourse and activism

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:37.869
didn't show a growing concern over Nazi

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.076
sympathizers and actual fascist movements in the

00:14:40.077 --> 00:14:41.380
interwar United States.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.989
But the critique was much more often centered on

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:46.509
the insistence, centered on critiquing that

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:48.519
insistence on gradualism you heard in the

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:49.884
article I quoted, the idea

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:53.919
that the country was moving slowly toward more

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:54.950
tolerance.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:57.099
And the response to that in light of what was

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:58.660
happening in Europe, in light of

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.539
everyone's feeling that things were about to get

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.149
much worse, the response was gradualism

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.150
isn't enough.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.659
And you can see a bit of that attitude in this

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.240
cartoon, which was published two weeks after

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:16.269
Kristallnacht and this cartoon

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.700
ran with the accompanying editorial,

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:21.249
"Colored America is also shocked.

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:22.929
But there are some things that Colored America

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.599
cannot understand about white America's reaction

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:26.950
to the atrocities.

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.529
They could not understand was humanitarian

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:32.470
support and widespread condemnation

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:34.964
without self reflection on the United States's

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.360
own history and present realities."

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.424
The article went on, "How could you be shocked

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:43.179
over ransacking when the United States had,

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:45.879
quote, robbed Indians of a whole continent?

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:48.099
How could you be shocked over these killings

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.290
when five thousand lynchings were on record?

00:15:50.739 --> 00:15:52.839
How could you be shocked over racial pollution

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:55.329
laws when similar legislation ordered

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:56.860
American society?"

00:15:59.709 --> 00:16:01.869
With the onset of war, and especially with the

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.209
US entrance into the conflict in late

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:06.928
1941, this argument gained a great deal of

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:08.300
political power.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.065
The United States was not yet a fascist state

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.719
within Black antifascist analysis, but

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.719
the pervasive presence of domestic Hitlerism,

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.059
as they often called it, put it in complicit,

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:21.729
dynamic relationship to the regimes of Italy,

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.489
Germany and Spain, as well as those of European

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:25.490
colonial rule.

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.582
This stance disputed the framing of the conflict

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:31.329
of World War II as one of democracy

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:33.486
versus fascism, freedom versus tyranny, good

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:34.700
versus evil.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.253
It challenged the idea that

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:39.819
the US racial order was an acceptable system,

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.930
emphasizing its hypocrisy, its vulgarity

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:43.929
and its inhumanity.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.899
This argument found its clearest expression in

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:48.762
the Double V campaign, which was first

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.669
championed by the Pittsburgh Courier and called

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:53.483
for victory over aggression, slavery,

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:56.919
and tyranny both abroad and at home.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:16:59.469
And in many cases, it was clear, it was made

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:01.959
clear that this meant double victories against

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.577
fascism, the American variety, as

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:05.890
well as the European brand, as

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:07.850
one writer put it.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:10.492
And it's my sense that at this point it's really

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.599
no longer about compare and contrast.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:16.459
Many, many simply

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.518
couldn't understand fascism as an entirely

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:19.530
foreign concept.

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.189
The lines were far too murky and

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.101
it wasn't this clear, you know, fascism

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.054
was not as clear opposite, or sort of other of

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:29.419
American democracy, but much more like a

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:30.420
doppelganger.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:32.861
And at times, of course, invocations of

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.629
Hitlerism did function as a simple

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.250
shorthand, or signifier, or insult.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:40.940
But the idea of a unique American fascism

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:42.435
had really taken hold and

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.129
it got to the heart of a lived experience

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:48.286
of racialized rightlessness within a liberal

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:49.320
democracy.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.709
And the scholars, Bill Mullen and Christopher

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:53.376
Vials recently expressed this idea

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:55.200
really well.

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:57.739
They wrote, "for people of color at

00:17:57.740 --> 00:17:59.798
various historical moments, the experience

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:02.547
of racialization within a liberal democracy

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.880
could have the valance of fascism.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:07.862
That is to say, while a fascist state in a white

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.069
supremacist democracy have very different

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:11.995
mechanisms of power, the experience

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:15.109
of racialized rightlessness within a liberal

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:17.809
democracy can make the distinction between it

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:19.547
and fascism murky at the level of

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:21.370
lived experience.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:24.559
For those racially cast aside outside of liberal

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.912
democracy's system of rights, the word 'fascism'

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.379
does not always conjure up a distant and alien

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:31.380
social order."

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:35.403
The idea of an incipient American fascism

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.699
as expressed through the Double V campaign,

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:41.905
raise the stakes of what would happen if they

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:44.079
fail to succeed on both fronts.

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:47.049
As one editorial from The Chicago Defender put

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:49.479
it in 1942, "all

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.685
of Negro America is revolted at the monstrous

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.186
atrocities Hitler is heaping upon the Jewish

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:55.280
people.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:57.693
We who are Negroes know that if Hitler wins, we

00:18:57.760 --> 00:18:59.829
too will be the victims of slaughter of even

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:00.880
greater magnitude.

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:03.666
If Hitler wins, the poll taxers of the South

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:06.331
who now use lynching as a weapon of terror

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.919
will use it as a weapon of extermination."

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:11.811
And as you can see from these images on

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.154
the screen, Double V was also rooted

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:17.699
in patriotism and win the war

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.112
gusto and it helped inspire and cohere

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.362
the official logic of patriotic antifascism

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:26.091
so central to the American war effort.

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:28.745
Wartime civil rights efforts really

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:31.491
marked a point of no return for federal

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:34.470
policies, federal support of racial liberalism.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:36.689
That's the moment you really start to see a sea

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.119
change regarding federal support

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:42.149
of policies of racial integration.

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.449
And civil rights activism during the war

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:48.239
also pushed many white Americans to

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.514
to really confront issues of racial inequality

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.911
in significant ways, giving rise to new

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.404
standards of tolerance and new terms

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.949
of acceptable language and behavior.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.459
And it's in this moment that Leon Bass

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:03.460
enlisted.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.119
Bass was born and raised in Philadelphia.

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.040
He grew up in predominantly

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:10.700
Black neighborhoods, went to mostly all Black

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.409
schools, and his parents, who were former

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.539
sharecroppers from South Carolina, they they

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:17.549
kept him, they seem to have kept him very

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:20.549
sheltered about the full force

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:22.800
and structural violence of Jim Crow

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:25.699
and racial violence in the United States.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.139
However, that all changed when he

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:29.560
when he joined up.

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.795
Twenty years old by the time the Second World

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:36.159
War had become a reality.

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:38.889
No, I was--I, I finished school

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:41.079
in 1943 and I was eighteen.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.160
And that's when I became part of the military.

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.381
And you, how did you become part of the

00:20:46.565 --> 00:20:47.770
military? Did you volunteer or get drafted?

00:20:49.089 --> 00:20:51.040
Yes. I went out and I, I volunteered

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.169
and I was to go out with the next group.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:55.929
And when I went down to the induction center,

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.256
institutional racism smacked me right in the

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:01.120
face, because

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:03.910
the sergeant was there and

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:05.889
he told me to go one way.

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:08.349
When I went through the door and he told my

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.509
white friends to go another way, because

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.800
my country practices promulgated and

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:16.719
promoted institutional racism

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.209
and the military was one of the largest to do

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.647
just that. And so, I went into an all-Black

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:23.983
unit save for the officers.

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:26.199
They were white, but

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.909
all of my comrades in arms at that time were

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:30.920
Black.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.647
Bass was promptly sent down south for basic

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:37.270
training.

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:39.989
Eight of the nine largest army training camps

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.509
were located in the South, so this was a common

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:44.039
experience for Black GIs.

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.349
And that also meant the majority of white

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.790
officers overseeing their training came

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:50.250
from the south as well.

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:52.829
And Bass found his time in basic training

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.500
extremely demeaning and disaffecting,

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:57.662
not only for the daily degradations of

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.152
Jim Crow, but also for being relegated

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:02.365
to a labor unit.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.870
Was it your perception, though, that the Black

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:10.639
troops generally, though, fully

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:14.459
understood the fact that while

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:16.859
the rhetoric of the war against Nazi racism and

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.830
so forth was fine, in practice,

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.239
the country was doing something entirely

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:21.539
different?

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.540
Yes, it was as though you were schizophrenic.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.310
Our country had, was two personalities you know.

00:22:27.510 --> 00:22:29.274
In one way we may make the wonderful

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:31.439
pronouncements. You know, we, we talk about our

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:33.719
Judeo-Christian ethics and we're going to make

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:35.339
the world a better place for democracy and all

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:36.390
that other jazz.

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:38.519
But then when you get down to the real thing and

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:40.870
you start seeing the way they operate, you

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:43.409
know, things were not, they were not in

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:45.030
consonance. And so I

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.719
began to be an angry, frustrated young

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:49.960
Black soldier.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:52.469
At this time, I wanted to get out of the army.

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:54.539
I'll be very honest with you. After my

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:56.647
experiences, I really did not want to be in

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.040
this man's army and.

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:03.849
Especially after having to stand on a

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:04.928
bus when there were no

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.485
seats at the back, having to stand up for 100

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:10.870
miles looking at empty seats, yeah,

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:12.869
that didn't endear me to, to my

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:14.870
country.

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:17.229
I couldn't eat in a restaurant.

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:19.119
I had to go around the back and knock on the

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:20.120
door to get food.

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:21.609
And I'm in a uniform.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:25.179
And I saw PWs, prisoners

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:26.797
of war from Germany being allowed

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:29.399
to go into restaurants and sit down to eat.

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.449
And yet I was not entitled to, for that same

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:32.450
opportunity.

00:23:35.449 --> 00:23:37.150
Bass shipped out for Europe in October

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.449
1944 as part of the 183rd Engineer

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:43.130
Combat Battalion of the US 3rd Army.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:45.969
Soon they arrived in Belgium, fought in the

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.489
Battle of the Bulge, which was the last major

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:50.679
German offensive campaign of the Western Front.

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:53.710
And as laborers, soldiers like Bass

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:56.199
were really first on the front lines and often

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:57.200
last to leave.

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:58.869
They built roads and bridges.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:00.585
They loaded munitions and supplies.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:02.469
They disabled landmines.

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:05.073
They transported the wounded and they tended to

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:06.080
the dead.

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.530
They crossed into Germany in March of

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:11.979
1945 and by April they had reached

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.439
the east central German city of

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:15.470
Weimar.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:20.419
Just to the north, in the hills above

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:21.983
the city of Weimar sat Buchenwald.

00:24:21.984 --> 00:24:23.062
The SS opened the camp

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:26.068
in 1937, primarily for political

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.509
prisoners, but they were soon joined by Jews,

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:31.764
Roma, Jehovah's Witnesses, military deserters,

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.219
prisoners of war and other groups.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.949
Between July 1937 and April

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.491
1945, it is estimated the SS imprisoned

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:42.310
some 250,000 people there.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.637
Buchenwald was one of 44,000 camps,

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:48.229
ghettos, and incarceration sites operated by

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:50.689
Nazi Germany and its allies across

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:53.000
Europe between 1933 and

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:54.750
1945.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:57.079
And the process of camp liberation itself

00:24:57.830 --> 00:24:59.300
was a chaotic affair.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.996
With US forces fast approaching, the Germans

00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:05.659
had attempted to evacuate some 30,000 prisoners,

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.389
roughly a third of whom they killed or died

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:09.390
in the process.

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:12.439
In the meantime, an underground resistance

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:14.743
network within the camp stormed the watchtowers

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:17.600
and seized control in anticipation of

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:19.609
the approaching Americans who arrived that

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:21.706
afternoon and found 21,000

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:23.160
people there.

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:24.582
In Bass's words:

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:30.449
We went to Weimar, set up our

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:31.479
bivouac area, and then

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.050
went immediately with an officer,

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:36.920
about five of us.

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:38.799
We were in the intelligence reconnaissance

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:41.379
section of our unit and we went right

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:42.719
to Buchenwald.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:47.050
And that was the day that I was to discover

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:50.079
what had really been going on in

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.689
Europe under the Nazis, because

00:25:52.690 --> 00:25:53.690
I walk through the gates

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:58.139
and I saw Walking

00:25:58.140 --> 00:25:59.140
Dead people.

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:02.193
Seriously, walking dead people.

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:04.629
I am not a doctor.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.669
I cannot assess things

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:09.745
that accurately. But in just looking at these

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:11.620
people who were skin and bone

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:13.610
and dressed in

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.439
those pajama type uniforms, their heads clean

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.329
shaved and

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:23.140
filled with sores due to malnutrition.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:25.420
And here they were coming towards us,

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:29.619
making all kinds of guttural statements

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:31.029
and using their own language.

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:33.400
And it was very difficult for me to comprehend

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:35.529
what was going on. I just looked at this in

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.780
amazement and, and

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:40.029
I said to myself, you know,

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:43.419
my God, you know, who are these people?

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:46.160
What have they done?

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:49.672
What was their crime? You know, it's hard for me

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:51.318
to try to understand why anybody

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:53.821
could have been treated this way, I don't care

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:56.290
what they had done and just didn't

00:26:57.100 --> 00:26:58.570
grab me. And I didn't have any

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:01.699
way of thinking, of putting a handle on it.

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:03.760
No frame of reference. I was only 20.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.975
And here you can see the moment that Bass was

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:11.899
just describing, he only spent

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:13.909
one day at Buchenwald, a matter of hours,

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.568
really, but the experience fixed

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:18.739
itself permanently in the back of his mind,

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:21.229
although for many years, decades, really,

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:22.820
he never spoke of it.

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:26.060
And he remained stationed in Germany long enough

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:29.119
to watch massive swastikas demolished

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.339
off of government buildings in the city of

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:33.251
Nuremberg, before he was shipped to the

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:35.930
Philippines, where he served for another

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:36.960
eight months.

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:42.844
Now, VE day in early May

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:45.248
1945 was jubilant, but

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:49.209
as of May 1945, color lines

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:51.760
snaked across every aspect of American life.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:55.149
White mobs, vigilante groups and innumerable,

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:57.388
innumerable iterations of local, state and

00:27:57.550 --> 00:28:00.219
federal officials police these lines

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:01.239
as they saw fit.

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:04.075
Jim Crow lawmakers vowed to defend the social

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.550
and political order at whatever cost.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:08.791
Even blood banks remained segregated, a

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:12.219
hold out of popular racial science so egregious,

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:14.949
so glaringly parallel to Nazi race law that

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:17.349
it became one of the most heated civil rights

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:19.000
issues of the war years.

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:21.819
Articles and man on the street interviews

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.025
published in the Black press in the immediate

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:25.902
wake of VE day indicate that Germany's

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.520
total surrender prompted questions about

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:31.149
whether fascism had really been defeated,

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:33.772
whether it could ever be defeated within Western

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:36.579
capitalist societies, and about

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.736
whether white Americans were poised to learn

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:40.872
anything from fascism, fascism's reign

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:42.770
and apparent ruin.

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.729
As one factory worker told her interviewer,

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:48.399
If the whites haven't learned by now, I guess

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:49.400
they never will.

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:56.069
And here you can see two political cartoons from

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.979
the summer of 1945, grappling

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:01.449
with clear,

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:04.189
obvious signs of

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.610
anti-Blackness and racism continuing

00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:10.280
as the war came to a close.

00:29:12.140 --> 00:29:14.209
And as the war came to a close, and really in

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:16.969
the months that followed, racial violence

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:20.089
did surge throughout the United States.

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:22.424
Right. Mobs of white vigilantes were targeting

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:24.710
returning Black veterans.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:27.739
You saw this deliberate stripping away of

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:30.215
wartime gains through a southern mobilization

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:32.780
in defense of Jim Crow.

00:29:34.460 --> 00:29:37.069
In the summer of 1945, in Congress,

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:39.979
lawmakers staged fierce campaigns

00:29:40.220 --> 00:29:41.935
on the floor of the Senate, arguing

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:45.259
against fair employment legislation and fought

00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:47.465
viciously against widely supported efforts to

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:49.541
eliminate the poll tax and pass federal

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:51.649
anti-lynching legislation.

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:54.169
And there's one really remarkable article from

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:56.032
that summer in which a Black columnist

00:29:56.900 --> 00:29:58.713
named Marjorie McKenzie observed that

00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:02.119
all of this racist and really explicitly

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:05.089
racist and explicitly antisemitic filibustering

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:07.344
that was happening at the Capitol garnered new

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:08.550
meaning when you

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:12.719
recognize that just a block away,

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:14.974
mere steps away, the Library of Congress, they

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:17.459
had opened an exhibit of mural sized

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:18.569
atrocity photos.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:21.599
These are scenes from, from concentration camp

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:22.600
liberation.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:26.393
And thinking about that juxtaposition, McKenzie

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:28.358
wrote, "it has said, it has been

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:31.514
said that is a good thing for Americans to see

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:34.000
the horrors of the German concentration camps

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:36.309
because seeing them will make us resolve never

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:38.659
to allow anything like that to happen here."

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:42.010
But McKenzie doubted such an assertion.

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:44.949
"The military collapse of fascism is going to

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:47.739
leave the world a heritage of fascists without

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:49.063
labels," she wrote.

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:52.252
"To defeat the organized strength of a system of

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:54.358
government without destroying the ideology

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:57.070
upon which it is based is dangerous."

00:30:57.670 --> 00:31:00.219
And her argument really

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.278
returns to this theme I already mentioned.

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:04.187
Her argument came down to the murkiness,

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:08.190
of the lack of demarcation line,

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:10.649
the instability of the demarcation line

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:13.379
and the vigilance that would be required to

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:16.260
determine where one form of politics

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:19.020
began and, and one,

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:21.590
or one, where one ended and one began, right.

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:23.903
And making that determination before it was too

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:25.030
late.

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:28.419
And for some, claims of American

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:30.219
fascism or a kind of unique form of American

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:32.170
fascism, they were largely rhetorical.

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:34.449
They were a provocative way to tie foreign

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:36.655
affairs to domestic issues and draw attention

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:38.289
to the civil rights cause.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:41.065
For others, however, the threat of US fascism

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:43.140
was possible and rather urgent.

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:45.639
And for many, I think it was some combination of

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:47.257
those two feelings, some strategy

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:49.449
and some dread.

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:52.989
And either way, they were rooted

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:54.729
in the understanding that white Americans

00:31:54.730 --> 00:31:57.159
refused to actually interrogate their history,

00:31:57.370 --> 00:32:00.459
their complicity, their insistence on reform

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:02.191
rather than reconstruction.

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:05.920
And early postwar commentary in the Black press

00:32:06.880 --> 00:32:08.889
that focused on the afterlife of fascism,

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:11.442
the idea that Hitler had been defeated

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:13.659
but his ideas had not.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.429
The realities and residues of those

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:19.889
ideas, they argued they were still

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:22.649
palpable in American life and

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:25.679
they included racial chauvinism and

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:28.679
nativist sensibility; exclusionary

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:31.229
nationalism defined through legal claims to

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:33.719
whiteness; lost cause nostalgia and

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.270
fantasies of white victimhood; a

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:37.658
militant and deputized

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.459
citizenry; the mass

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:43.949
caging of internal and external enemies

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:46.204
in reservations, ghettos and detention centers

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:47.800
and prisons.

00:32:48.299 --> 00:32:49.965
And after 1945, reckoning with all

00:32:50.819 --> 00:32:53.480
of this, African-Americans called for justice.

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:55.529
They called for reflection.

00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:58.048
But really, clearly, they called for vigilance.

00:32:58.049 --> 00:33:00.989
So they recognized that there was a danger

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:03.419
that fascism was still pending

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:06.509
in a world that had already shown

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:09.599
that they--it wasn't

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.548
going to come to terms with, with what it had

00:33:11.549 --> 00:33:12.549
just done.

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:16.109
And for Bass, it would take him some time

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:19.079
to, to, to put these connections together.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:22.469
Upon his return home, he sort of set to make a

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:24.449
life for himself. He started a family.

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:27.329
He got his PhD, he built his career and

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:28.919
he became involved with the civil rights

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:29.920
movement.

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:31.980
The assassination of Martin Luther King in

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:34.709
1968 had a profound impact on him.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.829
And in his testimony, he describes the heartache

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:40.589
and horror that he felt in the wake

00:33:40.590 --> 00:33:41.590
of that event.

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:44.279
This was a personal grief and disillusionment

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.559
for him personally, but it was something he saw

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:48.900
reflected back tenfold

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:51.779
in the young Black and brown students

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.150
at the high school where he served as principal.

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:57.179
So it was around this time to the late 60s,

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:58.993
right around 1970 in particular, that

00:33:59.730 --> 00:34:02.039
he started to think about Buchenwald

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:03.280
again.

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:08.240
And one day I came to a classroom,

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:11.179
and in that classroom there was a lady,

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:14.239
and she was trying to talk to the young men who

00:34:14.240 --> 00:34:15.410
were not listening to her.

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:17.929
You know, they were doing everything but

00:34:17.930 --> 00:34:18.930
listening.

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:21.409
And I stood at the door and I began to realize

00:34:21.410 --> 00:34:22.929
that she was a survivor and she

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:25.369
had survived one of the worst camps.

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:26.569
She had survived Auschwitz.

00:34:27.290 --> 00:34:29.642
And she was here today on this particular day to

00:34:29.660 --> 00:34:32.299
share her experience, her pain, if you will.

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:35.289
And these young men were not listening.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:37.620
They had their own pain, you know, and I

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:39.439
understood that, you know, the pain of

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:41.900
rejection, the pain of not giving, being

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:44.040
given the kind of recognition you deserve.

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:45.739
I understood all of that. I had been through

00:34:45.740 --> 00:34:46.818
there and I knew their

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:50.199
pain, but I also knew her pain.

00:34:51.740 --> 00:34:53.457
And so I had to say to them, hold it, fellas,

00:34:53.458 --> 00:34:54.458
cool it.

00:34:54.950 --> 00:34:57.529
Listen, what she says is

00:34:57.530 --> 00:34:58.789
true. I was there.

00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:01.819
And so they got quiet for a while.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:04.850
And once she started telling

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:08.060
her account of what happened at Auschwitz,

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:10.320
they really listened.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:12.709
And she told them how she lost her grandmother.

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:13.710
Grandfather.

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:17.769
Parents, brothers, sisters, cousins,

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:19.389
all those near and dear who had come into that

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:21.819
camp, all of them had gone to

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.400
the gas chambers and had ended up in the ovens.

00:35:25.270 --> 00:35:27.609
And of her family, she was the only one

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:29.240
that came out of there.

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:32.270
And they listened.

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:34.509
She shared with them so much

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:38.140
and they asked her questions, which made me know

00:35:39.100 --> 00:35:41.470
that she had to reach them.

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:44.199
They came up and looked at the numbers tattooed

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:46.630
on her arm and they

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:48.716
thanked her and they did something

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:51.329
I-- hadn't been done in a long while.

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:52.799
They left that room in silence.

00:35:54.340 --> 00:35:56.319
And, of course, she turned to me and of course,

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.231
tears were coming down her face and she

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:00.099
thanked me for my intervention.

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:02.529
And wanted

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:05.109
to know more about my experience

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:06.110
at Buchenwald.

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:09.650
And I began to remember.

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:17.109
Bass's own experiences with the exclusionary

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:18.384
racial order of the United

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:21.590
States and in later years, his awareness

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:24.723
of the inequalities that shape the world of his

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:27.269
students coming of age in the late sixties,

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:29.864
all this became the lens through which he came

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:32.369
to process what he saw at Buchenwald.

00:36:32.800 --> 00:36:34.956
He realized that the camp was not a singular

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:37.081
place, in his words, but rather evinced

00:36:37.870 --> 00:36:40.359
a culmination to white supremacy, the

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:42.069
face of evil, he called it.

00:36:42.370 --> 00:36:45.190
Racism, antisemitism, bigotry,

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:47.920
prejudice and all manner of hatred.

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:50.796
Bass concluded that Jim Crow and fascism did

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:52.710
stem from the same roots.

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:55.510
And like many before him, he felt the problem

00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:58.060
was that the United States had largely failed in

00:36:58.062 --> 00:37:00.849
the necessary project of probing those roots,

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:03.519
those that cut deep across Europe

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.671
and its colonies, as well as those that

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:07.948
stretched in hardened networks through its

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:11.109
own rich and bloody soil.

00:37:13.330 --> 00:37:14.499
And look what it did to us.

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:17.709
Racism has just about split us so many different

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:19.869
ways 'til we can't live in harmony with one

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:21.539
another, suspicious.

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:23.830
All of this I discovered

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:28.389
as an adult and I didn't get it when I was

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:30.669
young, when I should have had it, because people

00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:32.289
didn't want to deal with it. It was an ugly side

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:34.509
of our history and ugly things we want to push

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:35.859
under the rug. We don't want to deal with it.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:38.709
And I'm saying to people today, you must

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:40.220
face it.

00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:42.760
You can't laundry and sanitize our history.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:45.085
You must take history with its beauty and you

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:46.659
must take it with its degradation.

00:37:46.660 --> 00:37:49.269
You've got to deal with it for us to be whole

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:51.429
human beings and and to make a difference.

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:52.920
And this is especially true with young people.

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:55.444
And so I go around telling my story.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.408
And, of course, you know, I get the people

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:00.339
saying, Leon, why do you deal with this?

00:38:00.340 --> 00:38:02.319
This is, this is not a Black problem.

00:38:02.590 --> 00:38:04.452
I said, hey, it's not a Black problem.

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:05.660
It's not a white problem.

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:06.770
It's a human problem.

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:09.319
And we've got to face it.

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:11.819
And as Dr. King said, injustice

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:15.833
anywhere is the loss of justice

00:38:15.940 --> 00:38:18.279
everywhere. You see words to that effect

00:38:18.580 --> 00:38:20.830
and it's true. What affects you, affects me.

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:23.139
Your pain has to be my pain and my pain has to

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:25.719
be your pain. And I must somehow convey that to

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:27.969
the young people that I come to.

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:31.120
It's my commitment and somebody says

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:33.449
you, why do you deal with that mess?

00:38:33.460 --> 00:38:35.079
You know, I said, I'm sorry.

00:38:35.470 --> 00:38:37.577
I know it's been forty something years, but

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.309
that doesn't make it go away.

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:42.909
It only makes us become more aware that

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:45.579
we today have to do something that to stop

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:48.609
that which created the Final

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:51.400
Solution and that something

00:38:51.550 --> 00:38:52.550
is racism.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:56.049
Really, racism is that the root of all of

00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:58.157
this. Under that umbrella comes bigotry and

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.580
prejudice and discrimination, unemployment,

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:03.303
people who are unemployable, large institutions

00:39:03.820 --> 00:39:05.529
filled with those who are drug addicts and those

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:08.079
who are criminals, all because somehow we

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:09.424
haven't come to grips with that institution

00:39:09.425 --> 00:39:10.494
called racism.

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:13.498
And we have to because we see the ultimate

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:15.709
of racism, which was what I saw in Buchenwald.

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:17.990
And so what do we do?

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:20.502
Do we as educators,

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:22.900
and I use it in the broadest sense because we

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.630
all teachers of the young, in our

00:39:26.830 --> 00:39:29.589
way of life, do we convey to them

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:31.581
the best that we can be or do

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:34.395
we give them the worst side that we

00:39:35.140 --> 00:39:36.140
have?

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:39.009
Do we promote racism through

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:40.239
our apathy?

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:43.480
When we hear and see things, we say nothing,

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:46.539
because we don't want to jeopardize that which

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:48.849
is important to us. Our investment in a job,

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:50.859
our investment among friends.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:52.779
We don't want to disturb our families.

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:55.419
So no matter what people say or do,

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.263
just leave it alone, sweep it under the rug and

00:39:58.480 --> 00:39:59.500
somehow it'll go away.

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:01.539
But the skinheads are with us.

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:02.830
The Klan is with us.

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:04.629
It may be dressed in Brooks Brothers suit with

00:40:04.630 --> 00:40:05.630
attaché cases.

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.444
They may be in, in doctors, they may

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:10.326
be lawyers. They may be the guy who drives a

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:12.310
bus, but they are still with us.

00:40:15.010 --> 00:40:17.019
And just to conclude, the steady advance,

00:40:17.530 --> 00:40:19.509
a nationwide embrace of the civil rights

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:21.000
movement in the 1950s and '60s

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:23.470
did confirm that the fascistic

00:40:24.460 --> 00:40:26.469
filibustering of Mississippi senators had

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:28.314
been their swan song after

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:30.500
all.

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:32.551
These decades saw a substantial decline

00:40:33.190 --> 00:40:35.799
in the excessive mob rule and public expressions

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:38.079
of racism, public acts of racism and white

00:40:38.080 --> 00:40:40.540
supremacy so pervasive in the age before.

00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:43.539
Yet as Bass puts so well, many

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:46.869
of the currents of white supremacy,

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:49.449
but also the vast structures of race and class

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:52.089
inequality in the United States are still with

00:40:52.090 --> 00:40:54.640
us. And a significant part of the problem

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:58.059
are Americans to choose every day to buy

00:40:58.060 --> 00:41:00.549
into a myth that, a myth of

00:41:00.550 --> 00:41:02.259
the United States that for many people does not

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:03.260
exist.

00:41:04.389 --> 00:41:06.496
"White people were and are astounded by the

00:41:06.790 --> 00:41:09.309
Holocaust in Germany," wrote James Baldwin in

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:12.489
1962. "They did not know that they could act

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:14.940
that way, but I very much doubt whether Black

00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:17.237
people were astounded, at least in the same

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.529
way." And signaling a heritage

00:41:20.530 --> 00:41:23.020
of fascists without labels in the United States,

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:25.860
African-American voices in the 1930s and '40s

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:28.503
laid the groundwork for a longterm challenge to

00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:31.359
the patriotic antifascism and narrow myths

00:41:31.360 --> 00:41:33.909
of progress, which whitewashed national

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:36.000
memory in the postwar United States.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:38.973
They helped to forge an antiracist, antifascist

00:41:39.550 --> 00:41:41.461
counter heritage and radical organizing

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:44.049
tradition with tremendous coalition building

00:41:44.050 --> 00:41:46.010
possibilities, not only across the civil

00:41:46.540 --> 00:41:49.089
rights era and the age of Black power, but

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:50.770
also up into our present moment.

00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:52.700
Thank you.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:57.940
Thank you, Anna.

00:41:58.770 --> 00:41:59.770
Thank you.

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:02.549
We have a little bit of time for

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.489
questions, so if

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.780
anyone would like to ask

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:09.804
Anna a question, you can use the Q&amp;A

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:12.989
button or the

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:13.990
chat.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:15.561
I'll field both of those.

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:18.389
We do have one question already.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.109
And this is a question about the economic

00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:23.590
system.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:25.889
And the question is,

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.759
is capitalism to fascism as socialism

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:32.069
is to communism? Is our economic system one

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:34.199
reason it is so difficult for us to recognize

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:36.119
who we are? It's a big question, but to the

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:38.306
heart of it is our economic, is our economic

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:40.448
system or economics somehow tied

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:43.230
up with why we can't

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:46.259
we can't seem to come to terms with

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:48.089
racial inequality.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:52.429
To a very, to a very big question, I

00:42:52.430 --> 00:42:54.859
will offer a brief answer, which is yes,

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:58.400
right. And learning from the

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:01.819
Black radical theorists who were writing

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:03.170
explicitly about this.

00:43:05.470 --> 00:43:07.449
Over a hundred years ago, they would say that

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:09.520
the the entire formation of,

00:43:09.940 --> 00:43:12.292
of the American economic system was on the basis

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:14.292
of chattel slavery and, and plantation

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:16.509
economics, right? So they would say the two were

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:18.639
entirely caught up together and

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:21.310
anti-Blackness and racial formation was part of

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:23.799
the maintenance of capital and was the sort

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:25.956
of justification for capitalist accumulation

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:28.809
and expansion and extraction

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:30.339
of goods and colonies, right.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:31.960
So the simple answer is yes.

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:34.509
I think, I think they are entirely tied up

00:43:34.510 --> 00:43:35.510
together.

00:43:36.260 --> 00:43:38.779
Thanks. All right, Gil has a question

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:40.250
here, fairly long.

00:43:40.870 --> 00:43:42.409
I'm going to go ahead and read it because I

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:44.630
think it's worth reading in its entirety.

00:43:45.470 --> 00:43:47.989
So Gil found it fascinating that you highlight

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:50.179
how Black American commentators understood

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:52.639
European fascism has centered on racism

00:43:53.030 --> 00:43:55.159
rather than about authoritarian forms of

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:57.859
government, as it has been recently discussed

00:43:57.860 --> 00:43:58.860
here in the US.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:00.949
And he thinks that's an extremely important

00:44:00.950 --> 00:44:01.950
perspective.

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:05.659
You talk mainly about perceptions of

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:08.080
resemblance between European fascism and the US,

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:10.576
but I wonder if you can talk a bit about the

00:44:10.580 --> 00:44:12.679
history of actual legal relationship and

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:13.680
influence.

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:16.249
Just two examples to prompt discussion would be

00:44:16.250 --> 00:44:18.740
James Whitman's book, Hitler's American Model

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:22.250
and and the relationship between

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:24.710
Nazi race policy and Jim Crow.

00:44:24.740 --> 00:44:26.780
You talk a little bit about that in your talk.

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:29.719
And there's another, I think

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.690
Rabinbach, I believe, has an article about

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:34.849
how African-American intellectuals try to draw

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:37.609
on the new postwar concept of genocide coined

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:39.859
by Raphael Lemkin during the Holocaust,

00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:42.679
after the Holocaust, to fight lynching in the

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:44.640
South and how it became intertwined with

00:44:45.310 --> 00:44:47.809
the with continuing your point, Soviet

00:44:47.810 --> 00:44:50.480
depictions of the US as a fascist state.

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:54.179
But those are yeah, those are great questions, I

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:56.099
definitely like the undercurrents of this talk

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:58.169
and things that I sort of had to cut in order to

00:44:58.170 --> 00:44:59.619
make this fit the time.

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:02.189
So first of all, right, that's a little bit what

00:45:02.190 --> 00:45:03.339
I was trying to get at, right.

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:05.271
That as early as like, literally before

00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:07.758
the march on Rome in 1922,

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:11.097
the Black press is sort of having

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:14.428
debates like are the fascists like Klansmen?

00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:16.574
And oftentimes they would say no, because the

00:45:16.575 --> 00:45:18.179
Klans-- the fascists are a little more

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:19.429
dignified. Right.

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:21.059
Sort of. But, but

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:24.870
there was this feeling early on that was about

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:27.839
this compare and contrast that was pretty

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:30.770
abstract, that was about,

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:33.960
you know, parallels, similarities, echoes.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.424
But also from the beginning, they were looking

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:39.480
at moments of direct exchange and

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:41.759
alliances. Right. So it's, it was something like

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:43.709
just a few months after the fascists in Italy

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:44.710
came to power,

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:49.109
a leader in the Ku Klux Klan announced that he

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:50.999
wanted to forge an alliance with them in order

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:53.159
to bring the Klan struggle for white supremacy

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:54.229
to Europe. Right.

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:56.699
So you, so you do have direct

00:45:57.030 --> 00:45:59.400
admiration, borrowing and exchange.

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:02.639
You also, of course, have massive routes of, of,

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:04.980
of interaction in the field of eugenics.

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:07.919
And I think there is a very famous comment where

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:10.027
some American eugenicist said the Nazis are

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:11.609
beating us at our own game.

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:13.500
And, of course, you have the research that, that

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:14.670
James Whitman's book

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:18.850
puts so well. Right. That, that, the, the,

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:21.630
the Nazi government did study

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:24.059
the kind of elaborate systems of American

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:26.339
race law to try to figure out what did and did

00:46:26.340 --> 00:46:28.279
not work. And were actually quite perturbed.

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:29.399
I mean, recognize that

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:32.489
many things wouldn't work in the context of Nazi

00:46:32.490 --> 00:46:34.051
Germany, but learned a lot specifically about,

00:46:34.052 --> 00:46:36.392
um anti, anti--

00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:40.069
sort of racial terms of citizenship, so,

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:42.319
so the Nazis also learned a lot from the Johnson

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:44.329
Reed Act, the very restrictive immigration law

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:45.391
passed in the United States in 1924,

00:46:47.330 --> 00:46:48.799
and also miscegenation laws.

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:49.810
Right.

00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:52.819
So, so you do have these moments of direct

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:54.679
exchange, which I think are also fascinating

00:46:54.680 --> 00:46:57.190
because the archive is just full of

00:46:57.200 --> 00:46:59.936
people tracking the parallels on both levels,

00:47:00.050 --> 00:47:02.859
right, on the kind of more abstract level

00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:05.230
of parallel agendas, and on actual

00:47:05.890 --> 00:47:07.089
routes of exchange.

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:08.859
And really quickly to your second question, I

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:10.624
mean, that's, that to me is, is such

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:14.020
a key component in the wave of

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:15.849
sort of in the immediate postwar moment, there

00:47:15.850 --> 00:47:18.039
actually three different petitions submitted by

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:19.900
African-Americans to the UN.

00:47:21.510 --> 00:47:24.069
On behalf of-- so the first one by

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:25.779
a group called the National Negro Congress in

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:28.539
1946, that was calling for an end to

00:47:28.540 --> 00:47:31.269
discrimination. The second one by the NAACP

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:33.328
in 1947 to another kind of coming together

00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:36.339
a human rights convention.

00:47:36.490 --> 00:47:38.589
And then the last one was in

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:41.439
1951 by the Civil Rights Congress, specifically

00:47:41.440 --> 00:47:43.547
demanding the United States be charged with

00:47:43.600 --> 00:47:46.179
genocide. And I think everyone should read that

00:47:46.180 --> 00:47:47.499
petition, We Charge Genocide.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.059
It's extensive. It's two hundred and forty

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:49.989
pages.

00:47:49.990 --> 00:47:52.810
But, but it shows a very keen,

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:55.299
really brilliant, really sharp, but

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.760
no less provocative interpretation

00:47:58.090 --> 00:48:00.839
of the actual legal crime

00:48:00.850 --> 00:48:02.679
of genocide. Actually, Raphael Lemkin

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:05.109
disapproved of the petition.

00:48:05.110 --> 00:48:06.789
But I think that story, really you have to

00:48:06.790 --> 00:48:08.440
understand it in the context the Cold War.

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:11.440
And even to see how much the American public

00:48:11.740 --> 00:48:14.143
widely praised the 40--

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:16.870
the '46 and '47 petitions, but by

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:18.725
1951, condemned it across

00:48:19.930 --> 00:48:22.359
the board, right. And it's significant that that

00:48:22.480 --> 00:48:24.391
the National Negro Congress in 1946 and

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:27.129
the Civil Rights Congress in 1951 were both

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:29.710
directly affiliated with the Communist Party.

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:31.449
Right. And that is the kind of other

00:48:31.450 --> 00:48:32.859
undercurrent of this whole talk

00:48:34.690 --> 00:48:37.180
that the, we're talking about the heyday

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:38.702
of radical interracial trade

00:48:39.940 --> 00:48:42.243
unionism and communist organizing in the United

00:48:42.310 --> 00:48:44.319
States. But again, what I think is so

00:48:44.560 --> 00:48:47.380
fascinating about this archive is just explodes

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:49.569
past that pretty quickly.

00:48:49.570 --> 00:48:51.429
And I think what's really significant is after

00:48:51.430 --> 00:48:54.069
the Hitler-Stalin pact, which was such a huge

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:56.302
blow to the left, the language of antifascism

00:48:58.090 --> 00:49:00.219
lived on, right. It really continued to be a

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:02.859
popular vernacular related

00:49:02.860 --> 00:49:05.319
to but also unmoored from the

00:49:05.320 --> 00:49:07.119
kind of political and ideological commitments

00:49:07.120 --> 00:49:08.850
of, of the Communist International.

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:11.540
Great question.

00:49:12.550 --> 00:49:13.850
And great, great answer.

00:49:13.870 --> 00:49:16.749
So we just have a couple more questions

00:49:16.750 --> 00:49:19.119
and then I think we'll, we'll

00:49:19.180 --> 00:49:20.180
start to wrap things up.

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:22.136
This is also another big question,

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:26.079
but I think the talk, it's

00:49:26.380 --> 00:49:28.209
clear that the talk would bring a question like

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:29.210
this up.

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:31.749
It seems as if there should be great empathy and

00:49:31.750 --> 00:49:33.759
understanding between the American Jewish

00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:35.710
community and the African-American community.

00:49:36.610 --> 00:49:38.913
How would you explain, though, then, that there

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:41.799
is such times of moments of such animosity

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:43.600
and conflict between these two groups

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:46.959
in the past and the present?

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:49.329
Yeah, no, that's that's a great question.

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:50.330
And I think...we,

00:49:51.740 --> 00:49:53.599
can we go over, can we go I don't want can we go

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:54.439
past--?

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:54.979
Sure.

00:49:54.980 --> 00:49:56.609
OK, I don't we, I don't, I don't want to have to

00:49:56.840 --> 00:49:58.730
rush these things because it seems very serious.

00:49:59.780 --> 00:50:01.639
So, I mean, I think that's actually reason

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:03.600
enough to just go and sit and watch Leon

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:06.650
Bass's full testimony

00:50:07.040 --> 00:50:09.245
because what exists underneath the surface of

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:10.440
his whole,

00:50:12.310 --> 00:50:14.889
his whole oral history is that the interview

00:50:14.890 --> 00:50:17.319
was conducted in 1988, which

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:19.570
I think was this particularly heated moment

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:21.609
of tension and,

00:50:22.390 --> 00:50:24.459
eventually, with the Crown Heights riots, sort

00:50:24.460 --> 00:50:27.330
of outright violent clashing

00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:29.545
between Black Americans and Jewish Americans.

00:50:30.370 --> 00:50:31.370
Right. And.

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:36.170
I mean, I think my interest in this

00:50:36.890 --> 00:50:39.499
archive and all of these calls for

00:50:40.070 --> 00:50:42.177
solidarity, and all these calls for sort of

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:44.792
building a movement, an alliance of the interwar

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:47.590
period was in-- was

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:50.089
sort of in pursuit of a larger

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:52.609
story than what I had heard for many years of my

00:50:52.610 --> 00:50:55.069
education, right, of these tensions, of the

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:56.630
pervasiveness of

00:50:57.920 --> 00:51:00.165
anti-Black racism amongst Jews and antisemitism

00:51:00.890 --> 00:51:02.389
amongst African-Americans.

00:51:02.750 --> 00:51:04.579
Sometimes in the archive you do find

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:09.540
people writing editorials in the Black press

00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:12.079
saying, like, we have to stop this, right.

00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:14.582
Sort of condemning the explicit antisemitism

00:51:15.170 --> 00:51:17.299
of groups, right. And a lot of that was sort of

00:51:17.300 --> 00:51:19.959
rooted in local, I think, either

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:22.370
sort of, in the case of, of, of

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:23.920
particular cities, like New York,

00:51:25.450 --> 00:51:27.489
local issues, local tensions.

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:32.812
But I think an example I really like, so that,

00:51:33.430 --> 00:51:35.289
the book I mentioned, this is someone else asked

00:51:35.290 --> 00:51:37.569
about book recommendations and one of the ones I

00:51:37.780 --> 00:51:39.699
mention, which I think has such a beautiful

00:51:39.700 --> 00:51:40.869
example to think about this,

00:51:42.570 --> 00:51:43.570
Michael Rothberg, who wrote

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:47.453
this great called Multidirectional Memory, it's

00:51:47.790 --> 00:51:49.739
his attempt to try to understand how it's sort

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:50.740
of less...looking

00:51:53.870 --> 00:51:55.585
at Holocaust memory, decolonization

00:51:56.380 --> 00:51:57.799
and civil rights movements and events that

00:51:57.800 --> 00:51:59.809
happened at the same time, what are the moments

00:51:59.810 --> 00:52:01.849
of collaboration and cross referencing rather

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.210
than competition and his explanation for

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:06.740
least in the United States, why the competition

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:09.559
happens. As he uses the example, he was writing

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:10.939
a book when they were getting ready to open the

00:52:10.940 --> 00:52:13.099
National Museum of African-American History on

00:52:13.100 --> 00:52:13.939
the National Mall.

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:15.349
A lot of people were struck by the sort of

00:52:15.350 --> 00:52:16.350
symbol that

00:52:17.960 --> 00:52:20.810
that museum was taking up, the literal

00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:22.550
last plot

00:52:23.780 --> 00:52:25.430
of the National Mall.

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:27.889
And he said for, for racialized groups in the

00:52:27.890 --> 00:52:29.089
United States, for minorities in the United

00:52:29.090 --> 00:52:31.009
States, for groups who are subject to racial

00:52:31.310 --> 00:52:33.049
persecution and prejudice in the United States,

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:35.210
there's often a feeling that there is a limit

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:38.189
to the amount of tension and the amount of

00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:40.139
recognition that they can get, right, so there's

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:42.599
like a competition, competition a la real estate

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:44.489
for who can be represented, who can be

00:52:44.490 --> 00:52:47.159
protected, who can receive dignity.

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:49.979
And his argument is essentially, right,

00:52:51.390 --> 00:52:53.007
that, that justice and solidarity

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:56.644
and the pursuit of human dignity should not be

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:58.580
like real estate, right.

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:00.420
That it should be rooted in

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:04.109
collaboration. So I think, my research,

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:06.359
I've just been over the years has been so struck

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:07.680
by how many other

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:10.559
conversations were happening.

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:12.659
But I think it is significant and it's important

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:15.349
to recognize the violence

00:53:15.350 --> 00:53:16.720
and contention as well.

00:53:17.700 --> 00:53:19.589
And I think someone else had a book, I think

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:21.269
another, if you want to like, what was, do you

00:53:21.270 --> 00:53:23.099
want to get the specific book recommendations

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:25.589
stuff? And I'm surrounded by the books I used to

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:27.629
write this talk. I mean, I think to me like an

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:29.492
amazing classic place to start, truly,

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:32.850
the book that got me into all of this is

00:53:33.870 --> 00:53:36.329
Glenda Gilmore, Yale Professor Glenda

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:37.409
Gilmore's Defying Dixie.

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:39.480
Not only is it just

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:43.046
an incredible, extensive history, she's also

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:46.109
just such a beautiful writer and a brilliant

00:53:46.110 --> 00:53:47.110
scholar.

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:50.040
So I really strongly recommend this and then

00:53:51.180 --> 00:53:53.830
two more, that I just have all around me.

00:53:54.930 --> 00:53:57.131
One is Susan Pennybacker's

00:53:57.630 --> 00:53:59.220
From Scottsboro to Munich,

00:54:00.090 --> 00:54:02.579
which looks more at the Black diaspora and

00:54:02.610 --> 00:54:04.080
concentrates in Great Britain.

00:54:04.980 --> 00:54:06.449
And then finally, this just came out and this

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:07.710
was the book I quoted from,

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:09.940
the US Antifascism Reader.

00:54:10.410 --> 00:54:11.819
It's really amazing,

00:54:13.240 --> 00:54:15.899
these very recent collection of all sorts

00:54:15.900 --> 00:54:17.999
of different political perspectives, all sorts

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:19.529
of different arguments, all sorts of different

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:21.883
assessments from the 1920s

00:54:22.530 --> 00:54:23.530
up through the present.

00:54:31.490 --> 00:54:33.156
I dropped a couple of links to, to

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:34.950
those titles

00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:39.109
as well in the chat, but

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:40.825
you're welcome to get in touch with

00:54:41.690 --> 00:54:43.639
me, my email's on the website.

00:54:43.640 --> 00:54:46.099
And I can, I can provide you with

00:54:46.280 --> 00:54:48.049
more information about those readings, if you

00:54:48.080 --> 00:54:49.080
like.

00:54:49.460 --> 00:54:51.889
If there aren't any other questions,

00:54:52.250 --> 00:54:53.377
then I'd like to extend

00:54:54.770 --> 00:54:57.469
my thanks again to Anna for some

00:54:57.470 --> 00:54:59.000
really enlightening presentation.

00:54:59.720 --> 00:55:01.940
And thank you to everyone who came today.

00:55:02.330 --> 00:55:03.909
And we'll see you next time.

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:06.699
I thank you all so much and you're welcome to

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:09.009
send me an email if you want to, if you have

00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:10.567
questions or want to chat.

